            <h5 class="mt-5">
                What is Roundup?
            </h5>
            <p>Monsanto first released Roundup to market in the 1970s. It was a popular choice of herbicide for gardeners, farmers, agricultural workers, maintenance workers, and other people in need of a weed killer. One of its active ingredients is glyphosate, a non-selective that kills plants.</p>
            <p>Non-selective means that it kills nearly any plant indiscriminately. After application, the leaves absorb the chemical, passing it to the roots and throughout the plant. This prevents the plant from creating the required proteins that help it grow. Without these proteins, the plant eventually dies.</p>
            <p>In 2000, the patent Monsanto had for using glyphosate in Roundup had expired. Other companies started to manufacture and market similar herbicides, resulting in over 750 available in the United States by 2015. Products containing glyphosate are mainly sold as a liquid chemical, but some companies also sell it in salt and acid versions. People can use glyphosate on plants, vegetable crops, fruit, and more.</p>
            <p>The International Agency for Research on Cancer (IARC) reviewed the evidence about the potential for cancer to develop from glyphosate exposure and decided to classify it as probably carcinogenic to humans. The IARC based their decision on sufficient evidence to prove that pure glyphosate could cause cancer in animals and limited evidence proving that it might cause cancer in humans. They also determined that there was strong evidence that pure glyphosate and glyphosate formulations cause genotoxicity.</p>
            <h5 class="mt-5">Groups at Risk of Cancer from Roundup</h5>
